# libpdx-elevate — capability manifest (D4 per design/tooling/plan.md §6)
#
# Version:      1.1.0 (2026-09-11 signed release cut, LE.M1-003 / #21).
#               v1.0.0 (2026-08-22) covered the M1-M5 entry set; this
#               v1.1.0 pass adds every M6 + LE.M1-M7 entry point that
#               landed on `main` between 2026-08-25 and 2026-09-11 (see
#               the CHANGELOG's [1.1.0] section for the wave-by-wave
#               list; per-entry cap sets are itemised below).
# Kind:         shared library (paideia-as .pdx sources; no bin/, no lib/*.so)
# Consumer:     any tool that requests a cap from svc.elevate-broker via the
#               client-side helper set in src/elevate_client*.pdx.
#
# libpdx-elevate is a library, not a tool: it holds no caps itself.  What it
# declares here is the *floor* set of caps the *consuming process* must hold
# for each entry point below to succeed.  pkg (§5.1), the R50 coreutils,
# and every future tool that needs a per-op elevate hop link this manifest
# into their own caps.decl via a `+use libpdx-elevate` line at exec time.
#
# The kernel's cap-check runs against the consuming process's cap set — this
# file is documentary: it tells `pkg install libpdx-elevate` what to prompt
# the caller to grant, and it tells `paideia-as release --sign` what to hash
# into manifest.pdxsig.  It does NOT relax any check the kernel enforces.

+entry elevate_client_lookup_broker
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read, "svc.elevate-broker")
  # svc_lookup returns the broker endpoint id after checking the name-service
  # cap.  No mint required — the endpoint is already registered.

+entry elevate_client_send_req
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, broker_ep_id)
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(mint,  reply)
  # broker_ep_id comes from lookup_broker; reply is minted by the caller for
  # the APR return channel.  The mint right is per-invocation and narrowed
  # to a single-frame endpoint.

+entry elevate_client_recv_reply
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read, reply_ep_id)
  # reply_ep_id must be the caller's own reply endpoint.  No cross-process
  # read is possible: endpoint_take_pending checks holder identity.

+entry elevate_client_journal_req
+entry elevate_client_journal_apr
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, uej)
  # uej_append (UEJ_KIND_ELEVATE=5) fans out to /system/audit/user-events/.
  # M3-001 wires directly to the kernel primitive; when libpdx-audit.M2
  # lands the caller may substitute a libpdx-audit cap for this line.
  #
  # LE.M6-001 (#33) note: the four call sites (journal_req/_apr/_op and
  # cap_attest below) now additionally invoke _elevate_client_audit_file_
  # append, a STUB helper reserving the file-sink call slot for the
  # /system/audit/elevate.log line write.  The stub body is currently a
  # bare `ret` so no additional cap is required TODAY.  When libpdx-audit
  # #32's audit_file_append lands and the stub swaps to a real transport,
  # one new line will be added under each of these four entries:
  #     KIND_PDXFS(write, "/system/audit/elevate.log")
  # (or whatever cap-kind libpdx-audit#32 settles on).  Adding it now
  # would document a cap the shipping code never exercises.

+entry elevate_client_cap_attest
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, uej)
  # LE.M5-001 (#31): uej_append (UEJ_KIND_ELEVATE=5, body0=ELVJ_EVT_ATTEST=4)
  # records a delegation-with-attestation event. Same audit-fanout cap as
  # journal_req/_apr/_op above -- no additional cap for the row-stamp half
  # (elevate_client_cap_bind_last_audit is a pure shadow-map write).

+entry elevate_client_cap_mint
+entry elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(mint,   row_id)   # M2-003
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(revoke, row_id)   # cap-lifetime enforcement
  # 0x191, derived over KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT (base 5).  Rights narrowed to the
  # specific row_id the caller obtained from the broker's APR reply.

+entry elevate_client_cap_revoke_cascade
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(revoke, row_id)
  # LE.M7-001 (#35): force-revokes row_id and every transitive shadow-map
  # descendant. Same single cap as _check_and_revoke above -- every row it
  # touches is one this process already held a KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL revoke
  # right over (its own row_id, or a row derived from one via
  # elevate_client_cap_derive, which mints each child under the SAME
  # caller-supplied broker context). No union with elevate_client_cap_mint's
  # entry: this function never mints.

+entry elevate_client_cap_reap_expired
+entry elevate_client_shutdown
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(revoke, row_id)
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, uej)
  # LE.M4-002 (#30): idle-time sweep of the 16-slot shadow deadline map
  # (reap_expired) and its teardown-time composition with reset
  # (shutdown = reap + reset). Union of _check_and_revoke's cap (revokes
  # one kernel row per expired shadow slot) and the audit-fanout cap
  # (per successful reap it emits one ELVJ_EVT_REAP record via
  # elevate_client_journal_reap -- best-effort, but the cap must still
  # be held for the write to succeed).

+entry elevate_client_cap_drain_broker_exp
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read, reply_ep_id)
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(revoke, row_id)
  # LE.M7-002 (#36): union of elevate_client_recv_reply's read-own-reply-
  # endpoint cap (drain_broker_exp polls the SAME kind of endpoint, just
  # non-blockingly) and elevate_client_cap_revoke_cascade's cap above,
  # which it calls once per drained ELV_OP_EXP frame.

+entry elevate_client_request_ex
+entry elevate_client_request_ex_j
+entry elevate_client_request_ex_r
  # Full-flow entry points: they compose the individual entries above.  The
  # caller must hold the *union* of the caps listed above for every entry
  # the composed path reaches.  The retry variant (_r) may loop the
  # send/recv/journal triple up to elevate_client_retry_get_max_attempts()
  # times per invocation; the cap set does not grow with retries.

+entry elevate_client_require
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(revoke, row_id)
  # ENH-002 (#13): per-op re-assert. Only cap this entry can exercise is the
  # kernel revoke elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke fires on an expired
  # row -- no broker hop, no mint, no IPC endpoint use.

+entry elevate_client_journal_op
+entry elevate_client_require_j
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(revoke, row_id)
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, uej)
  # ENH-004 (#16): audit-wrapped per-op re-assert. Union of elevate_client_
  # require's cap (above) and uej_append's write-to-audit-fanout cap
  # (same line elevate_client_journal_req/_apr already declare).

+entry elevate_client_require_scoped
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(revoke, row_id)
  # ENH-003 (#15): elevate_client_require plus a scope match and a budget
  # consume, both pure shadow-map reads/writes with no additional cap
  # beyond what elevate_client_require itself already needs.

+entry elevate_client_request_ex_ctx
+entry elevate_client_request_ex_j_ctx
+entry elevate_client_request_ex_r_ctx
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read, "svc.elevate-broker")
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(mint,  reply)
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, broker_ep_id)
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read,  reply_ep_id)
  # ENH-006 (#17): explicit-context twin of elevate_client_request_ex. Same
  # cap set as that entry -- only the source of target_fp_lo/timeouts
  # differs (a caller-owned ctx_buf instead of process globals), which
  # carries no capability implications.
  #
  # LE.M1-002 (#20): _ex_j_ctx / _ex_r_ctx extend the same discipline up
  # through the audit-wrapped (journal) and retry-wrapped stack levels
  # respectively.  _ex_j_ctx dispatches through _ex_ctx (same caps as
  # above) after journalling REQ; _ex_r_ctx dispatches through _ex_j_ctx
  # (identical cap set) on each retry.  The retry variant may loop up to
  # elevate_client_retry_get_max_attempts() times per invocation; the cap
  # set does not grow with retries -- same discipline as the legacy _ex_r.

+entry elevate_client_acquire
+entry elevate_client_acquire_ctx
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read, "svc.elevate-broker")
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(mint,  reply)
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, broker_ep_id)
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read,  reply_ep_id)
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, uej)
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(mint, row_id)
  # LE.M1-002 (#20): _acquire_ctx is the 7-arg ctx-carrying twin of the
  # 6-arg _acquire.  Same cap set (the ctx swap threads through _ex_r_ctx
  # rather than _ex_r; mint + shadow-bind are unchanged).
  #
  # ENH-001 (#11): composed request_ex_r + mint + shadow-bind returning a
  # row_id handle.  Union of every entry it reaches, PLUS the caller's own
  # pre-existing KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT cap over the broker at the cap-table slot
  # named `parent_ep_slot` in the caller's mint_ctx_buf -- that cap is NOT
  # minted by this entry point (it must already be held; see kind_elevate_
  # channel.pdx's parent-gate note in paideia-os). target_cap_kind /
  # target_cap_rights in mint_ctx_buf are opaque to this manifest -- they
  # name a DOWNSTREAM capability this elevate channel governs, declared by
  # whatever the caller links this library into, not by libpdx-elevate.

# Notes for downstream tools:
#
# 1. libpdx-elevate itself never holds KIND_SUPERVISOR.  Every cap it names
#    above is either narrowed to a specific object (broker_ep_id, reply,
#    row_id) or opaquely brokered through svc.* lookup.  This preserves the
#    audit chain: the elevate broker owns the policy table and the sign-off
#    trail, the caller owns the request and the returned cap.
#
# 2. No cap declared here is minted with lifetime > the request's requested
#    duration.  Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL>'s bounded-lifetime self-
#    invalidation (M2-003, tests/elevate_client_cap_test.pdx) means a
#    caller cannot silently retain a granted elevate past its deadline.
#
# 3. When libpdx-cap.M2 lands and elevate_client_cap_narrow_stub is
#    replaced with a real cap_narrow_rights call, one new line will be
#    added here:  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(narrow, row_id).  Deferred to
#    the v1.1.x follow-up that flips LE.M2-001 to LANDED (this v1.1.0
#    cut leaves the PENDING entry in `deps.list` untouched -- see the
#    dep-status note at the top of `deps.list` for the swap-site trail).
